WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.940
SPEAKER 1: Google
Cloud Next 2017

00:00:01.940 --> 00:00:05.385
hosted a large number of talks
detailing the future of cloud.

00:00:05.385 --> 00:00:07.760
If you didn't get a chance to
catch the session "Managing

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:10.220
Container Engine Clusters
Across Multiple Teams,"

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:12.080
then stay tuned, because
here's the recap.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:15.286
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:18.640
As development teams
embark into Kubernetes,

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:21.670
they will inevitably need to
revisit their project, cluster,

00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:24.910
and namespace organization
as new teams need access,

00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:26.530
or as they prepare
for production

00:00:26.530 --> 00:00:27.895
into multiple environments.

00:00:27.895 --> 00:00:30.270
SPEAKER 2: This brings up the
ever-common question of how

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:32.229
do I configure my environment.

00:00:32.229 --> 00:00:35.240
In practice, we see three
typical configurations.

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:37.630
SPEAKER 1: First is a shared
project, shared cluster,

00:00:37.630 --> 00:00:39.580
and different namespace,
where namespaces

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:41.410
map to different teams.

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:43.750
The benefit of this is that
it gives you potential cost

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:45.520
savings in proper isolation.

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:49.730
But now, resource isolation
is limited to CPU and memory.

00:00:49.730 --> 00:00:52.300
SPEAKER 2: The second is one
project, different cluster,

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:55.120
different namespace setup, which
gives you improved resource

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:55.780
isolation.

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:58.870
But you might lose
management benefits.

00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:01.300
SPEAKER 1: Third is different
project, different cluster,

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:03.670
and different
namespace, which offers

00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:05.710
greater isolation
and billing control,

00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:08.920
but again, lost
management benefits.

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:10.720
SPEAKER 2: But after
a lot of discussions,

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:12.520
one of the most
powerful setups has

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:15.490
to be doing a different project
for production, separating

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:18.400
dev test and production,
along with shared clusters

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:21.085
and different namespaces
for each team.

00:01:21.085 --> 00:01:22.960
SPEAKER 1: Now, once
you have things isolated

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:24.430
the way you like,
you can optimize

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:26.620
for resources offered
to them to optimize

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:28.300
your quality of service.

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:31.270
By specifying CPU and memory
limits on your containers,

00:01:31.270 --> 00:01:33.310
the scheduler can give
you a different quality

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:35.830
of service based on
resource requests

00:01:35.830 --> 00:01:38.980
for your pod, either
best effort, burstable,

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:40.486
or guaranteed.

00:01:40.486 --> 00:01:41.860
SPEAKER 2: Of
course, you now run

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:44.290
into the problem about what
the resource requests should

00:01:44.290 --> 00:01:45.310
be set to.

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:46.846
Without a strong
history of data,

00:01:46.846 --> 00:01:48.220
there's a big
chance you're going

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:50.350
to get these numbers
wrong for a while.

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:52.300
To help with that,
Kubernetes has a feature

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:54.340
called initial
resources, which looks

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:56.920
at the history of resource
usage for a container

00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:58.810
and sets the request
automatically based

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:01.570
on that history so that
once you've run a container,

00:02:01.570 --> 00:02:04.910
you don't need to specify the
request again in the future.

00:02:04.910 --> 00:02:07.780
The system essentially learns
what the right resource request

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:10.500
is by observing it
across multiple runs.

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:13.000
SPEAKER 1: For more information
about how to properly set up

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:14.583
your Kubernetes
environment, including

00:02:14.583 --> 00:02:16.540
some demos and other
insights, check out

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:18.285
the full talk on YouTube.

00:02:18.285 --> 00:02:19.660
If you're looking
for more recaps

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:21.539
